      "textEn": "123\n\nIt has to be returned. As explained in the Bhagavad Gita (7.19)—Bahunam Janmanante Jnanavanmam\nPrapadyate. The main thing is to consider Shrikrishna Vasudeva as everything and to be dedicated to him. The worker\ndoes not know this, but the devotee engaged in devotion to God knows action and knowledge in a perfect manner,\nTherefore, a pure devotee does not have any interest in action or knowledge. Knowing that which is devoid of desire,\nAnd which is not covered by karma. The true devotee is not even touched by action and knowledge. His life's only\npurpose is to serve God. And the mind, due to karma, is engaged.\nWith ignorance, it is attached to the self.\nBy the love of the Lord, one is not freed from the body.\nBy the yoga of the body, one is not liberated. 6. Meaning of the word\n\nEvam—in this way; Manah—mind; Karmavasham—subject to action; Prayunkhe—acts; Avidyaya—ignorance or lack of knowledge; Atmani—in the self; Upadhiyamane—remains covered; Preetiha—love; Na—not; Yavat—until; Mayi—to me; Vasudeva—Vasudeva or Krishna; Na—not; Mucchyate—gets rid of; Deha-yogena—through body contact; Tavat—until then. When the soul remains covered by the quality of ignorance, then it neither understands the soul nor the supreme soul,\nand his mind remains subjugated by desire-driven actions. Therefore, until he has love for me,\nVasudeva, he does not get rid of accepting bodily bondage. Meaning: When the mind remains contaminated by desire-driven actions, the soul desires progress from one state to another. Generally, every person remains engaged in rigorous work day and night to improve their financial condition. Even knowing the Vedic rituals, man desires to reach heaven.\nHe forgets that his real interest lies in returning to the Bhagavad-dharma. While performing desirable actions,\na human wanders in various wombs throughout the universe. But until he comes into contact with the Guru,\nhe does not engage in the service of Lord Vasudeva. To understand Vasudeva, one has to take many births.\nOne has to take. As confirmed in Bhagavad Gita (7.19)— Vasudeva is everything, yet he is supremely rare among great souls. Suffering for many lifetimes, one finds refuge in the lotus feet of Vasudeva. When that happens,"
